{"reference":"2 Chronicles 7","book":"2 Chronicles","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"This chapter brings the account of Solomon’s wisdom and temple building to its culmination: the temple is dedicated amid fire, offerings, worship, celebration, and a divine response. The sequence presents the temple’s completion not as an isolated building project, but as the fulfillment of the preparations associated with David and the setting for God’s promise and warning to Solomon and Israel.","verse_refs":["2 Chronicles 7:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_chronicles_007_0d2233e5c4d622b5c987e93d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Promise and Warning","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The divine answer holds together two possibilities for the covenant people. Humbling themselves, praying, seeking God, and turning from wicked ways are connected with forgiveness and healing; turning away to serve other gods is connected with being uprooted from the land and the temple’s ruin. The dedication therefore ends with a warning that the continued significance of this house is not presented as unconditional.","verse_refs":["2 Chronicles 7:11-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_chronicles_007_1cd60a510cf83286fdec3d0d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}